============
Installing NTL or compiling it from source
============

# This has only been tested on Ubuntu 26.04
# This is valid as of 2026, libraries may change


# NOTE:
# Install GF2X first, then NTL.

# See https://libntl.org/doc/tour-unix.html
# and especially https://libntl.org/doc/tour-gf2x.html


# To install the compiler, etc.
sudo apt install build-essential
sudo apt install m4

# To install:
sudo apt install libntl-dev


# To compile from source instead:

mkdir libntl.org
cd libntl.org

git clone https://github.com/libntl/ntl

cd ntl/src

# Note:
# ************* make check will fail if you don't do this, because it needs to find libgf2x.so
# /usr/local/lib is NOT in the default library search path in Ubuntu
# To add it, do:
echo "/usr/local/lib" | sudo tee -a /etc/ld.so.conf.d/local.conf
sudo ldconfig

# See https://libntl.org/doc/tour-gf2x.html, we must specify NTL_GF2X_LIB=on

./configure NTL_GF2X_LIB=on
make
make check

# NOTE:
# The above only creates a static .a library, not a shared .so library
# (whereas the package libntl-dev does create a shared library)

# This will install in /usr/local
sudo make install

# If you want to make sure the installed package doesn't interfere:
sudo apt remove libntl-dev
